  <section id="what" aria-labelledby="what-h">
    <h2 id="what-h">What Is FFMI?</h2>
    <p>The <strong>fat-free mass index</strong> is a height-adjusted measure of how much lean mass you carry: muscle, bone, organs and water. Because it leaves out fat, it shows muscularity in a way BMI cannot. Two men can both have a BMI of 27; one may have an FFMI of 19 and 25% body fat, the other an FFMI of 23 and 12% body fat.</p>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/ffmi-chart-men-women.webp" width="1600" height="720" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="FFMI chart for men and women with categories from below average to rarely natural"><figcaption>FFMI categories for men and women.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section id="chart" aria-labelledby="chart-h">
    <h2 id="chart-h">FFMI Chart for Men and Women</h2>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Normalized FFMI categories</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">Men</th><th scope="col">Women</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Below average</th><td>under 17</td><td>under 14.5</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Average</th><td>17&ndash;20</td><td>14.5&ndash;17</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Trained</th><td>20&ndash;22</td><td>17&ndash;18.5</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Very muscular</th><td>22&ndash;23.5</td><td>18.5&ndash;20</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Elite natural</th><td>23.5&ndash;25</td><td>20&ndash;21.5</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Rarely reached naturally</th><td>over 25</td><td>over 21.5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>Average ranges come from Kyle and colleagues&rsquo; study of almost 5,700 healthy adults; the upper bands reflect what trained lifters reach. Category names are a practical guide, not medical labels.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="formula" aria-labelledby="formula-h">
    <h2 id="formula-h">FFMI Formula</h2>
    <p class="tp-note" style="font-size:16px!important"><span class="tp-kbd">Lean mass = weight &times; (1 &minus; body fat %)</span><br><span class="tp-kbd">FFMI = lean mass (kg) &divide; height (m)&sup2;</span><br><span class="tp-kbd">Normalized FFMI = FFMI + 6.1 &times; (1.8 &minus; height in m)</span></p>
    <ol class="tp-steps"><li><strong>Weigh yourself.</strong> Weigh yourself in the morning, before eating, in light clothing.</li><li><strong>Estimate body fat.</strong> Use a DEXA scan, calipers, a smart scale or the U.S. Navy tape method. The calculator can estimate it from your neck and waist.</li><li><strong>Find lean mass.</strong> Multiply your weight by (1 minus your body fat as a decimal). At 80 kg and 15% body fat, lean mass is 80 × 0.85 = 68 kg.</li><li><strong>Divide by height squared.</strong> Divide lean mass in kg by height in meters squared. 68 ÷ (1.78 × 1.78) = 21.5.</li><li><strong>Normalize for height.</strong> Add 6.1 × (1.8 − height in meters). For 1.78 m, add 0.12, giving a normalized FFMI of 21.6.</li></ol>
  </section>

  <section id="limit" aria-labelledby="limit-h">
    <h2 id="limit-h">The Natural FFMI Limit</h2>
    <p>In 1995, Kouri and colleagues measured 157 male athletes, 83 drug-free and 74 who had used anabolic steroids. The drug-free men topped out around a normalized FFMI of 25, while many users went beyond it. Later work has found a few drug-free athletes slightly above 25, so it is a rough ceiling rather than a test.</p>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Weight at 12% body fat for a given FFMI</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Height</th><th scope="col">FFMI 20</th><th scope="col">FFMI 22</th><th scope="col">FFMI 25</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 6 in</th><td>136 lb</td><td>150 lb</td><td>171 lb</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 10 in</th><td>157 lb</td><td>173 lb</td><td>197 lb</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">6 ft 2 in</th><td>181 lb</td><td>199 lb</td><td>226 lb</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>Most men who train hard for years land between 22 and 24. Reaching the upper end usually takes a large frame as well as time; see <a href="/average-wrist-size/">average wrist size</a> and the <a href="/body-frame-size-calculator/">frame size calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="bodyfat" aria-labelledby="bodyfat-h">
    <h2 id="bodyfat-h">FFMI Depends on Your Body Fat Estimate</h2>
    <p>FFMI is only as accurate as the body fat number behind it. A 3-point error in body fat moves FFMI by about 0.6 to 0.8. DEXA is the most accurate common method; tape measures and smart scales are rougher. Track change over time with the same method, and use the <a href="/body-fat-percentage-chart/">body fat percentage chart</a> to sanity-check your number against pictures.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="improve" aria-labelledby="improve-h">
    <h2 id="improve-h">How to Raise Your FFMI</h2>
    <p>FFMI rises only when lean mass rises. That means progressive resistance training for all major muscle groups, enough protein (about 1.6&nbsp;g per kg of body weight per day is a common evidence-based target), a small calorie surplus or maintenance, and sleep. Beginners can gain several kilograms of lean mass in the first year; gains slow to a fraction of that after a few years.</p>
    <p>See how your build compares in other ways with the <a href="/average-bicep-size/">average bicep size</a>, <a href="/shoulder-to-waist-ratio-calculator/">shoulder-to-waist ratio</a> and the <a href="/body-visualizer/">3D body visualizer</a>.</p>
  </section>

